WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:07.829
Hi, this is Presh Talwalkar. This math problem is being shared around Facebook and one of the recent post has over two million comments.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:14.200
I've seen this post being shared since December, but for some reason It's getting really popular right now,

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.449
and the mainstream media is talking about how this problem is baffling the Internet.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:24.000
So I wanted to address the problem in this video and discuss what many people believe to be the correct answer.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.060
Can you figure it out?

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:36.380
So we can consider this as a math problem, if you consider the apple, the banana, and the coconut as different variables and not having numerical values.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:36.880
 

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.600
We'll start out with the first equation that 3 apples are equal to 30.

00:00:42.309 --> 00:00:47.159
If we divide both sides by 3, we get the result that 1 apple is equal to 10.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.579
This is generally an accepted part of the problem.

00:00:52.149 --> 00:00:57.538
We'll use the information, the apple, in the second equation, and this is where things get a little more debatable.

00:00:57.969 --> 00:00:59.969
So the apple is equal to 10...

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:04.000
and then if you look at the rest of the equation, you have 2 different bunches of bananas.

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:11.698
Now some people consider 1 whole bunch of banana as one variable, but I would consider counting each banana separately.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:16.920
So there're actually 4 bananas in each bunch, so you'd want to count that there are an 8...

00:01:17.799 --> 00:01:20.188
...total of 8 different single bananas.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:29.040
And that equals 18. So we subtract by 10 and then divide by 8 we get the result that each banana is equal to 1.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.040
 

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:36.659
We now have the equation that 4 bananas minus a full coconut is equal to 2.

00:01:37.119 --> 00:01:40.229
So we have 4 minus the coconut equals 2.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:44.579
We can solve this to get that the coconut is equal to 2.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:51.240
Now even people who agree up to this point still might have a debate about the very last line,

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.900
which is what you're supposed to evaluate.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Because if you look closely there's some differences in our pictures.

00:01:56.619 --> 00:02:04.829
We don't have a full coconut, we actually have a half coconut. So the full coconut is equal to 2, then a half coconut would be equal to 1.

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:13.079
So we have 1 for the coconut, we add 10 for the apple, and finally people still get confused at this point because we don't have...

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.650
...4 bananas. If you look closely, we actually only have three bananas. So you want to add three at this point,

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:24.779
and you get 1 plus 10 plus 3, and that gives us our answer of 14.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.300
This is what most people consider to be the correct answer.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.390
You could interpret the pictures to be slightly different.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:36.029
You could say the coconut might be... say 3/4 of the coconut, in which case you get a slightly different answer.

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:39.119
But 14 is what many people consider to be the correct answer.

